1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,310
One last time, before the law, a word about words.

2
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:26,050
Take the word blind. A blind corner. A blind spot. A blind date. Blind faith. Not
one of those is about a person's eyes, and yet you understood every one of them.

3
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:36,484
Words do that. They carry more than one meaning, and we pick the right one without
noticing we have chosen. This series has followed four words.

4
00:00:36,534 --> 00:00:48,034
Three of them you had probably never asked about. The fourth is the one the series
is named for, and in this last law it is used in a way we have not met yet.

5
00:00:48,084 --> 00:01:01,750
Not of a man. Of a lamb, at the head of a list of things that are wrong with it.
Brian read that list and reached for a word of his own. The word is broken.

6
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:09,775
The last law in this series is not about blind people. It is about lambs.

7
00:01:09,825 --> 00:01:17,170
It is here because the list would not be honest without it, and because Brian found
it the hardest of the four to read.

8
00:01:18,220 --> 00:01:23,690
You are not to present to the LORD any animal that is blind, injured, or maimed.

9
00:01:24,873 --> 00:01:35,730
One last time in this series, Mister Scaredy Cat. Brian still will not use his own
voice, so this one speaks for him. Every word is his.

10
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:46,170
[Brian] This one was harder to read than I expected, and not because of what it asks
of a lamb. It is because it puts blindness straight into the category of broken.

11
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:52,476
Broken. That is the word this episode has to answer.

12
00:01:52,526 --> 00:02:03,190
We will answer it with two Hebrew words, a market in the Temple, a prophet's test,
and a look back over everything the Law said about the blind.

13
00:02:04,740 --> 00:02:14,484
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

14
00:02:14,534 --> 00:02:23,021
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

15
00:02:23,071 --> 00:02:33,586
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so. This episode is part of our series
on the Law: what God commanded about the blind.

16
00:02:33,636 --> 00:02:36,838
In these episodes there are no imagined scenes.

17
00:02:36,888 --> 00:02:45,570
Everything you hear is from the text, from the old books read in their own voice,
from the Hebrew words themselves, and from dusty old Brian.

18
00:02:48,370 --> 00:02:58,330
First, where we are. Three places and a thousand years. Leviticus chapter
twenty-two, at Sinai, gives the rule for what may be offered.

19
00:02:58,380 --> 00:03:07,926
Deuteronomy chapter fifteen, on the plains east of the Jordan forty years later,
applies it to the firstborn of the flock, which belonged to God.

20
00:03:07,976 --> 00:03:19,532
And Malachi, the last book of the Old Testament, speaks in Jerusalem a thousand
years after Sinai, to priests of the second Temple who were offering blind,

21
00:03:19,582 --> 00:03:24,323
lame and sick animals and calling the LORD's table contemptible.

22
00:03:24,373 --> 00:03:27,840
Leviticus chapter 22, verses 17 to 25.

23
00:03:28,270 --> 00:03:34,795
[Reader] Then the LORD said to Moses, “Speak to Aaron and his sons and all the
Israelites and tell them,

24
00:03:34,845 --> 00:03:45,938
[Reader] ‘Any man of the house of Israel or any foreign resident who presents a gift
for a burnt offering to the LORD, whether to fulfill a vow or as a freewill
offering,

25
00:03:45,988 --> 00:03:53,551
[Reader] must offer an unblemished male from the cattle, sheep, or goats in order
for it to be accepted on your behalf.

26
00:03:53,601 --> 00:04:00,229
[Reader] You must not present anything with a defect, because it will not be
accepted on your behalf.

27
00:04:00,279 --> 00:04:08,306
[Reader] When a man presents a peace offering to the LORD from the herd or flock to
fulfill a vow or as a freewill offering,

28
00:04:08,356 --> 00:04:12,099
[Reader] it must be without blemish or defect to be acceptable.

29
00:04:12,149 --> 00:04:22,521
[Reader] You are not to present to the LORD any animal that is blind, injured, or
maimed, or anything with a running sore, a festering rash, or a scab;

30
00:04:22,571 --> 00:04:27,953
[Reader] you must not put any of these on the altar as a food offering to the LORD.

31
00:04:28,003 --> 00:04:40,199
[Reader] You may present as a freewill offering an ox or sheep that has a deformed
or stunted limb, but it is not acceptable in fulfillment of a vow.

32
00:04:40,249 --> 00:04:51,479
[Reader] You are not to present to the LORD an animal whose testicles are bruised,
crushed, torn, or cut; you are not to sacrifice them in your land.

33
00:04:51,529 --> 00:05:04,170
[Reader] Neither you nor a foreigner shall present food to your God from any such
animal. They will not be accepted on your behalf, because they are deformed and
flawed.’”

34
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:17,031
Blind heads the list in verse twenty-two, as it did in the list of the priest's
defects. Now Deuteronomy, and what became of a blind firstborn.

35
00:05:17,081 --> 00:05:20,850
Deuteronomy chapter 15, verses 19 to 23.

36
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:29,657
[Reader] You must set apart to the LORD your God every firstborn male produced by
your herds and flocks.

37
00:05:29,707 --> 00:05:37,095
[Reader] You are not to put the firstborn of your oxen to work, nor are you to shear
the firstborn of your flock.

38
00:05:37,145 --> 00:05:44,911
[Reader] Each year you and your household are to eat it before the LORD your God in
the place the LORD will choose.

39
00:05:44,961 --> 00:05:54,461
[Reader] But if an animal has a defect, is lame or blind, or has any serious flaw,
you must not sacrifice it to the LORD your God.

40
00:05:54,511 --> 00:06:02,848
[Reader] Eat it within your gates; both the ceremonially unclean and clean may eat
it as they would a gazelle or a deer.

41
00:06:02,898 --> 00:06:06,630
[Reader] But you must not eat the blood; pour it on the ground like water.

42
00:06:07,430 --> 00:06:12,980
Eat it within your gates, as you would a gazelle or a deer.

43
00:06:13,030 --> 00:06:23,485
Matthew Henry, a pastor in England who wrote his commentary on the whole Bible
beginning in seventeen hundred and six, puts it in one sentence:

44
00:06:23,535 --> 00:06:27,819
it was killed and eaten at their own houses as common food.

45
00:06:27,869 --> 00:06:35,140
The blemish kept it from the altar, not from the table. Remember that. Brian will
come back to it.

46
00:06:36,690 --> 00:06:49,476
Now the words, and this law has two, a negative and a positive. The negative is one
you heard last time. The animal must not have a "moom," a blemish.

47
00:06:49,526 --> 00:06:59,762
It is exactly the word of the blind priest. The priest with a "moom" does not
approach; the lamb with a "moom" is not accepted.

48
00:06:59,812 --> 00:07:10,473
The Law used the same measure for the man and for the lamb, and in the end, as the
encyclopedia notes, for Christ, the lamb without blemish.

49
00:07:10,523 --> 00:07:24,899
The positive word is "tah-meem." The animal must be "tah-meem," which the Berean
text gives as unblemished and Strong's as entire, whole, complete.

50
00:07:24,949 --> 00:07:29,440
It is the Passover lamb's word. Exodus chapter 12, verse 5.

51
00:07:29,990 --> 00:07:36,980
[Reader] Your lamb must be an unblemished year-old male, and you may take it from
the sheep or the goats.

52
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:49,262
But here is what the dictionary cannot tell you and the Bible can. "Tah-meem" is a
word used of people far more often than of animals. Listen.

53
00:07:49,312 --> 00:07:52,070
Deuteronomy chapter 18, verse 13.

54
00:07:52,620 --> 00:07:56,330
[Reader] You must be blameless before the LORD your God.

55
00:07:56,980 --> 00:08:02,530
Blameless. "Tah-meem." Psalm chapter 15, verse 2.

56
00:08:03,080 --> 00:08:09,510
[Reader] He who walks with integrity and practices righteousness, who speaks the
truth from his heart,

57
00:08:10,060 --> 00:08:22,322
With integrity. "Tah-meem." So the thing the altar asked of a lamb is the thing God
asks of a person's walk. Whole.

58
00:08:22,372 --> 00:08:29,340
And in neither case does it have anything to do with eyes. A blind man can walk
"tah-meem."

59
00:08:29,390 --> 00:08:37,450
That is the whole of the Law's answer to the word defect, and it is the beginning of
the answer to Brian's word, broken.

60
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:49,064
Now the old books, and a connection you may not expect. This law is why there was a
market in the Temple.

61
00:08:49,114 --> 00:08:55,090
Here is Edersheim, writing in eighteen eighty-three, on what happened when a
worshipper arrived with an animal.

62
00:08:55,660 --> 00:09:02,410
[The old books] When an animal was brought, it had to be examined as to its
Levitical fitness by persons regularly qualified and appointed.

63
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:11,270
Qualified examiners, who charged a fee. And so, Edersheim says, a shortcut appeared.

64
00:09:11,820 --> 00:09:20,728
[The old books] All trouble and difficulty would be avoided by a regular market
within the Temple-enclosure, where sacrificial animals could be purchased,

65
00:09:20,778 --> 00:09:26,375
[The old books] having presumably been duly inspected, and all fees paid before
being offered for sale.

66
00:09:26,425 --> 00:09:33,930
[The old books] Most improper transactions were carried on, to the taking undue
advantage of the poor people who came to offer their sacrifices.

67
00:09:35,080 --> 00:09:43,599
He gives a case from the rabbis of a pair of pigeons run up to the price of a gold
coin. Now think back to episode five.

68
00:09:43,649 --> 00:09:51,871
The tables Jesus overturned in the Temple court, on the day the blind and the lame
came to Him there, were this market.

69
00:09:51,921 --> 00:10:01,750
The rule that God be given the best had become a trade that took the most from the
poor. The law was good. What people built on it was not.

70
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:12,201
Which brings us to Malachi, who saw the same thing happen a thousand years after
Sinai, and four hundred before the tables were overturned.

71
00:10:12,251 --> 00:10:22,270
His argument builds by steps, and the order matters. Hear it whole. Malachi chapter
1, verses 6 to 14.

72
00:10:22,820 --> 00:10:31,797
[Reader] “A son honors his father, and a servant his master. But if I am a father,
where is My honor?

73
00:10:31,847 --> 00:10:45,086
[Reader] And if I am a master, where is your fear of Me?” says the LORD of Hosts to
you priests who despise My name. “But you ask, ‘How have we despised Your name?’

74
00:10:45,136 --> 00:10:56,204
[Reader] By presenting defiled food on My altar. But you ask, ‘How have we defiled
You?’ By saying that the table of the LORD is contemptible.

75
00:10:56,254 --> 00:11:08,951
[Reader] When you offer blind animals for sacrifice, is it not wrong? And when you
present the lame and sick ones, is it not wrong? Try offering them to your governor!

76
00:11:09,001 --> 00:11:18,239
[Reader] Would he be pleased with you or show you favor?” asks the LORD of Hosts.
“But ask now for God’s favor. Will He be gracious?

77
00:11:18,289 --> 00:11:24,855
[Reader] Since this has come from your hands, will He show you favor?” asks the LORD
of Hosts.

78
00:11:24,905 --> 00:11:32,559
[Reader] “Oh, that one of you would shut the temple doors, so that you would no
longer kindle useless fires on My altar!

79
00:11:32,609 --> 00:11:39,945
[Reader] I take no pleasure in you,” says the LORD of Hosts, “and I will accept no
offering from your hands.

80
00:11:39,995 --> 00:11:46,160
[Reader] For My name will be great among the nations, from where the sun rises to
where it sets.

81
00:11:46,210 --> 00:11:56,846
[Reader] In every place, incense and pure offerings will be presented in My name,
because My name will be great among the nations,” says the LORD of Hosts.

82
00:11:56,896 --> 00:12:09,576
[Reader] “But you profane it when you say, ‘The table of the Lord is defiled, and as
for its fruit, its food is contemptible.’ You also say: ‘Oh, what a nuisance!’

83
00:12:09,626 --> 00:12:22,528
[Reader] And you turn up your nose at it,” says the LORD of Hosts. “You bring
offerings that are stolen, lame, or sick! Should I accept these from your hands?”
asks the LORD.

84
00:12:22,578 --> 00:12:32,282
[Reader] “But cursed is the deceiver who has an acceptable male in his flock and
vows to give it, but sacrifices a defective animal to the Lord.

85
00:12:32,332 --> 00:12:38,690
[Reader] For I am a great King,” says the LORD of Hosts, “and My name is to be
feared among the nations.

86
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:54,613
Now go back through it, one step at a time. It starts with family. A son honors his
father, and a servant his master. So if God is a father, where is His honor?

87
00:12:54,663 --> 00:13:04,669
If He is a master, where is the respect? Then the charge. The priests have called
the LORD's table contemptible.

88
00:13:04,719 --> 00:13:10,030
They bring blind animals, and lame ones, and sick ones, and lay them on the altar.

89
00:13:11,080 --> 00:13:19,119
Then the test, and it is a sharp one. Try offering that to your governor. Would he
be pleased with you?

90
00:13:19,169 --> 00:13:28,093
Matthew Henry puts the question plainly: would these priests dare to insult an
earthly ruler like that? Of course not.

91
00:13:28,143 --> 00:13:34,390
No one brings a sick animal as a gift to the man who governs him. Yet they brought
it to God.

92
00:13:36,076 --> 00:13:49,024
Then the weariness. You say, what a nuisance, and you turn up your nose at it. The
worship itself has become a chore. And last, the curse.

93
00:13:49,074 --> 00:13:53,522
It does not fall on the poor man who had nothing better to give.

94
00:13:53,572 --> 00:14:04,450
It falls on the man who had a good animal in his flock, promised it to God, and then
brought a blemished one instead. Henry sums up what the prophet is doing.

95
00:14:04,980 --> 00:14:14,210
[Matthew Henry] The prophet is here, by a special commission, calling the priests to
account, though they were themselves appointed judges, to call the people to an
account.

96
00:14:14,760 --> 00:14:24,077
In other words, the priests were the ones meant to judge the people's offerings. Now
God judges theirs.

97
00:14:24,127 --> 00:14:34,348
And Henry adds that what is said to the priests is said to all of us. The blind
animal in Malachi is not about blindness.

98
00:14:34,398 --> 00:14:40,870
It is the sign of a gift given with contempt, by people who had something better and
kept it back.

99
00:14:42,045 --> 00:14:50,670
That last point is the key to this whole law. God was never really measuring the
animal. He was measuring the giver.

100
00:14:52,060 --> 00:15:04,774
The rest of the Bible keeps the same measure. It never asks how much a gift was
worth. It asks what the gift cost the one who gave it. You can hear that in two
places.

101
00:15:04,824 --> 00:15:14,010
The first is the very first offering in the Bible, from two brothers, Cain and Abel.
Genesis chapter 4, verses 3 to 5.

102
00:15:15,560 --> 00:15:27,417
[Reader] So in the course of time, Cain brought some of the fruit of the soil as an
offering to the LORD, while Abel brought the best portions of the firstborn of his
flock.

103
00:15:27,467 --> 00:15:40,150
[Reader] And the LORD looked with favor on Abel and his offering, but He had no
regard for Cain and his offering. So Cain became very angry, and his countenance
fell.

104
00:15:40,700 --> 00:15:50,568
Abel brought the best of the firstborn of his flock. Cain brought some of what he
had. The second place is King David.

105
00:15:50,618 --> 00:16:04,090
A man named Araunah offered him a threshing floor, and oxen for the sacrifice, as a
free gift. David would not take them. 2 Samuel chapter 24, verse 24.

106
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:15,821
[Reader] “No,” replied the king, “I insist on paying a price, for I will not offer
to the LORD my God burnt offerings that cost me nothing.”

107
00:16:15,871 --> 00:16:20,990
[Reader] So David bought the threshing floor and the oxen for fifty shekels of
silver.

108
00:16:22,490 --> 00:16:30,280
Burnt offerings that cost me nothing. David would not give God something that had
cost him nothing.

109
00:16:30,580 --> 00:16:42,422
A thousand years later, Jesus sat in the Temple, opposite the treasury, and watched
people putting in their money. Many rich people put in large amounts.

110
00:16:42,472 --> 00:16:53,570
Then a poor widow came and put in two small copper coins. Mark chapter 12, verses 43
to 44.

111
00:16:54,120 --> 00:17:04,174
[Reader] Jesus called His disciples to Him and said, “Truly I tell you, this poor
widow has put more than all the others into the treasury.

112
00:17:04,224 --> 00:17:12,150
[Reader] For they all contributed out of their surplus, but she out of her poverty
has put in all she had to live on.”

113
00:17:12,700 --> 00:17:21,715
All she had to live on. That is the measure the Law was teaching with every
offering. God never looked first at the size of the gift.

114
00:17:21,765 --> 00:17:23,850
He looked at the heart of the giver.

115
00:17:24,900 --> 00:17:27,010
Brian, on the word broken.

116
00:17:27,560 --> 00:17:37,101
[Brian] This one was harder to read than I expected, and not because of what it asks
of a lamb. It is because it puts blindness straight into the category of broken.

117
00:17:37,151 --> 00:17:48,357
[Brian] In society's terms I am disabled. But when I hear of a disabled car, I can
swap in the word broken without losing anything, and I most certainly do not feel
broken.

118
00:17:48,407 --> 00:17:53,096
[Brian] I do most tasks differently now, with a different set of skills and more
steps.

119
00:17:53,146 --> 00:18:00,344
[Brian] Yet I still carry nearly all the same obligations, responsibilities,
enjoyments and pleasures that I had with full sight.

120
00:18:00,394 --> 00:18:04,670
[Brian] If anything, I am better at the things God wants me to be better at.

121
00:18:04,720 --> 00:18:14,514
[Brian] Fewer distractions mean more focused attention on the people I love, and on
the people I need to go and find to love. The law itself leaves me a door.

122
00:18:14,564 --> 00:18:17,934
[Brian] The blind firstborn was not thrown away.

123
00:18:17,984 --> 00:18:28,466
[Brian] Deuteronomy says to eat it at home, as you would a gazelle or a deer, and
Matthew Henry says what that means: not fit for the altar is not the same as
worthless.

124
00:18:28,516 --> 00:18:34,034
[Brian] A thing with a defect still fed a family. I would not have chosen the word
defect for myself.

125
00:18:34,084 --> 00:18:42,710
[Brian] But I notice that even this law, the hardest one in this series for me to
read, does not call the blind animal broken. It calls it ordinary.

126
00:18:45,260 --> 00:19:00,017
One last word, and it is the one we planted in episode seven. Malachi ends his
charge with these words: My name is to be feared among the nations. That is
"yah-ray."

127
00:19:00,067 --> 00:19:16,427
To fear, or to revere. It is the word of the stumbling block law: you shall fear
your God. The first law about the blind and the last both rest on it. And look at
why.

128
00:19:16,477 --> 00:19:26,205
Nobody would see the stone placed in the road. Nobody at the altar could tell, once
the animal was offered, whether a better one had been kept back at home.

129
00:19:26,255 --> 00:19:35,930
Both laws were written for the wrong that no one catches, and both give the same
reason to keep them. God saw.

130
00:19:37,230 --> 00:19:48,098
Now look back over the whole series, one law at a time. First, the stumbling block,
in episode seven. The word was "mik-shole."

131
00:19:48,148 --> 00:19:55,911
Brian asked whether it meant a trip or a fall, and the word answered: a fall, and
worse.

132
00:19:55,961 --> 00:20:02,300
That law was written for the people who do not catch themselves, and against the man
who thought nobody saw.

133
00:20:03,350 --> 00:20:11,689
Second, the servant's eye, in episode eight. Two words: "ah-yin," the eye, and
"khof-shee," free.

134
00:20:11,739 --> 00:20:21,291
It was the one price the Law ever put on an eye, and the price went to the man who
lost it.

135
00:20:21,341 --> 00:20:28,020
Not silver, but freedom, the same freedom as the seventh year, given that very day.

136
00:20:29,570 --> 00:20:44,892
Then the blind priest, in episode nine, and the blind animal, in this one. They
share one word, "moom," a blemish, and one answer to it, "tah-meem," whole.

137
00:20:44,942 --> 00:20:52,143
The same measure kept both the priest and the lamb from the altar.

138
00:20:52,193 --> 00:21:01,360
But "tah-meem" turned out to be a word for how a person walks, and a blind man can
walk whole as well as anyone.

139
00:21:03,570 --> 00:21:13,146
And holding all four together is one more word: "yah-ray," the fear of God.

140
00:21:13,196 --> 00:21:25,612
It was there at the beginning, in the stumbling block law, and it is here at the
end, in Malachi. Four laws. Not one of them calls a blind person broken.

141
00:21:25,662 --> 00:21:33,404
One protects him by name. One frees him. One keeps him in the priesthood and at the
table.

142
00:21:33,454 --> 00:21:42,045
And one, about lambs, says that God is given the best, and then lets the blind
animal feed a family.

143
00:21:42,095 --> 00:21:53,360
Read as a whole, the Law's word on the blind is not a word about what they lack. It
is a word to everyone else, about how to treat them, and about who is watching.

144
00:21:57,410 --> 00:22:08,320
This has been the Law. The stories of the Old Testament are still to come: Isaac,
Jacob, Eli, Samson, and the rest.

145
00:22:09,870 --> 00:22:20,006
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at
blindnessinthebible.com.

146
00:22:20,056 --> 00:22:31,471
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

147
00:22:31,521 --> 00:22:37,900
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

